        "original_id": "84b30fa0-adc8-5713-8e87-d4b3f788e8c8",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Plants: Myrsine, Styrax\n- Common names: matipo, kōlea, storax, snowbell\n- Places: New Zealand, Hawaiʻi, Asia, South America, Northern Hemisphere\n\nFacts:\n- Myrsine contains about 200 species.\n- Myrsine includes notable radiations such as the matipo of New Zealand and the kōlea of Hawaiʻi.\n- The New Zealand plant called black matipo, Pittosporum tenuifolium, is not related to Myrsine.\n- Styrax is commonly called storax or snowbell.\n- Styrax is a genus of about 130 species of large shrubs or small trees in the family Styracaceae.\n- Styrax is mostly native to warm temperate to tropical regions of the Northern Hemisphere.\n- Most Styrax species occur in eastern and southeastern Asia, and the genus also extends across the equator into South America.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
